WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.635
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:06.080
I hope some gaming nomads find
their way back to performance.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.692
Should we have stopped
to ask for directions?

00:00:08.692 --> 00:00:09.650
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.539
Nomad Socialite was a
new geolocation game

00:00:15.539 --> 00:00:17.330
where players traveled
all around the world

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:19.850
to rack up points, catch
the latest in urban events,

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.910
and meet a whole community,
all at the same time.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:25.222
The game has been a hit,
but their event producers

00:00:25.222 --> 00:00:26.930
noticed that the
analytics system-- which

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.230
was powered by Datastore--
started to slow significantly

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:31.340
when updating.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.830
See, any time you're trying
to store analytics data

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:36.140
in Datastore, I can
tell you the problem

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.300
without even
checking-- contention.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.490
Simply put, Datastore
contention occurs

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:44.420
when a single entity or entity
group is updated too rapidly.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:45.950
Once contention
gets high enough,

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:49.220
Datastore will queue concurrent
requests to wait their turn.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:52.160
But if the number of edits
on an entity is too high,

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.890
things will eventually slow
down as the requests stack up,

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:57.320
until timeout errors are thrown.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.270
Specifically, we
see this most often

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.220
when using Datastore
for things like counting

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.500
the number of views to
a page on your site.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.810
Or, in the case of
Nomad Socialite,

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:07.940
updating the analytics
data for an event

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.670
meant keeping a running count of
the number of users logged in,

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.770
and all of their
related data sets.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.670
Now, when this happens, there's
lots of ways to address it,

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.900
but the simplest
might be sharding.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:20.270
Sharding effectively
duplicates an entity,

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.940
and uses a version of
manual load balancing

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.070
to write updates to
the clones instead

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:26.910
of all to the same item.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.630
The result is that each clone is
partially updated with respect

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:30.620
to your data set.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.010
When you read from the sharded
set, you fetch all copies

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.912
and sum them together
on the client.

00:01:35.912 --> 00:01:37.370
You'll end up with
the same result,

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:38.852
but with less contention.

00:01:38.852 --> 00:01:40.310
To show the
performance difference,

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:43.550
we wrote a small test using the
Artillery framework in order

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:45.500
to simulate their user base.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.210
The graph here shows
pretty much what

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:49.670
we expected-- our sharded
version outperforms

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.410
its regular counterpart.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.080
Since sharding distributes
the work across more entities,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.200
the contention is lower,
thus performance is better.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.217
If you're interested in
how to set up sharding,

00:01:59.217 --> 00:02:00.800
or some other options
in case sharding

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.330
doesn't suit your
needs, check out

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:04.050
the article linked in
the description below.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:06.174
And don't forget to check
out the rest of the Cloud

00:02:06.174 --> 00:02:08.017
Performance Atlas
content, because when

00:02:08.017 --> 00:02:10.100
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

